> That's my point; it's an arms race, and a sufficiently determined
> player will always win if it's possible to.  I think only by
> randomizing prices (not entirely, just enough to confuse more tiers of
> items) can this be overcome.

There are other tricks available; one of them is to base the prices on
more information than the player knows "in-game".  Right now the racial 
stuff is an obvious piece of information that the player can get "in game", 
and he can do math accordingly from there; it's just more of a pain, and
it's not something you can count on to be a precise way anymore since
different shopkeepers are different, period.

What if, for example, prices fluctuated according to supply?  The stuff
that hadn't been generated yet would be correspondingly more expensive;
and since the player doesn't know what's been generated yet (by
definition, since he's trying to figure out what things are), it could
be one way to slide prices around.

I'm not saying this is necessarily the best way to go, just that
outright making prices totally random isn't the only way to solve the issue.

